---
/**
 * CtaPanel — the closing CTA panel.
 *
 * Recreates design/README.md §8 (CTA panel) and the approved reference in
 * design/design-reference/Basset Landing.dc.html exactly: a tinted rounded
 * card that pairs an H3 + muted body on the left with the site's primary
 * "Talk to us about the evaluation" mailto button on the right. The panel
 * itself is the visual break between §7 (What Basset is not) and the
 * footer — the brief specifies a tinted panel and does not mention a
 * separator hairline, so this section deliberately omits the 1px top
 * border every other section carries. To match the reference's rhythm
 * exactly, the section wrapper also omits the section-top padding: the
 * previous section's bottom padding provides the space above the panel,
 * and the wrapper's own bottom padding provides the space before the
 * footer (equivalent to the reference's margin: 0 0 clamp(48px,6vw,72px)
 * on the panel itself).
 *
 * The panel is a flex row on wide viewports: text stack on the left,
 * button on the right. flex-wrap: wrap collapses the row to a single
 * column below ~640px per the brief — the copy stacks above the button,
 * and flex-shrink: 0 on the button keeps its label from being squeezed.
 * The primary button matches the hero's button exactly (same colors,
 * size, weight, padding, radius, hover, and focus outline) so the site's
 * two CTAs are visually identical.
 *
 * Every color, size, spacing, radius, and copy string here is bound by
 * the brief; values live in src/styles/tokens.css and are read through
 * the token custom properties so a single change ripples across the
 * site. QA verifies against design/README.md §8 at 1440x900 (row: copy
 * left, button right) and 390x844 (single column: copy above button),
 * and confirms there is no horizontal scroll at 320px.
 *
 * No screenshots or product imagery — this section is text-only per the
 * brief.
 */
---

<section class="cta-panel-section" aria-labelledby="cta-panel-heading">
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="copy">
      <h3 id="cta-panel-heading" class="cta-panel-heading">
        Bring us a bug that cost you days.
      </h3>
      <p class="body">
        Basset is in development and we are taking a small number of early partners. You bring real tickets with logs, we prove it on your hardest cases, and early partners pay roughly half the standard price for the first year.
      </p>
    </div>

    <a class="btn-primary" href="mailto:michel@snappautomotive.io">
      Talk to us about the evaluation
    </a>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Section wrapper — the tinted panel below is the visual break, so this
     section deliberately omits the 1px hairline top border every other
     section carries (per the brief's §8, which describes a tinted panel
     and no separator). The wrapper also omits the section-top padding
     so the total spacing above the panel matches the reference exactly:
     the previous section's bottom padding provides the gap above, and
     this wrapper's bottom padding provides the gap before the footer.
     Left-aligned block, matching every other section. */
  .cta-panel-section {
    padding: 0 0 var(--section-padding-y);
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* Panel — the tinted card. Colors, border, radius, and padding come
     straight from the brief's §8 tokens (--color-cta-fill,
     --color-cta-border, --radius-cta-panel). flex-wrap: wrap lets the
     row collapse to a single column below ~640px so the copy stacks
     above the button on narrow viewports; align-items: center vertically
     centres the button with the copy at the wide breakpoint;
     justify-content: space-between pushes the button to the trailing
     edge on wide layouts. */
  .panel {
    background: var(--color-cta-fill);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-cta-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-cta-panel);
    padding: clamp(28px, 4vw, 56px);
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 32px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
  }

  /* Copy column — the H3 + body stack. flex: 1 1 320px lets the copy
     grow to fill the panel width when there's room and forces the row
     to wrap (dropping the button onto its own line) once the copy's
     minimum ideal width can't fit alongside the button. min-width: 0
     lets the H3 wrap inside a shrinking flex item instead of forcing
     the panel wider than its container at narrow widths. */
  .copy {
    flex: 1 1 320px;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* H3 — the global h3 rule in BaseLayout already applies the type
     tokens (family/size/weight/line-height/letter-spacing); we reset
     the top margin to 0 so the panel padding is the sole source of
     vertical rhythm above it, and pin ink color defensively so a
     global tweak cannot mute it. */
  .cta-panel-heading {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* Body paragraph — 15.5px/25px on the CTA panel's muted ink
     (#41564a) per the brief. margin-top matches the design-reference's
     16px value, and max-width: 52ch caps the measure at the value the
     brief calls out. */
  .body {
    margin: 16px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 25px;
    color: var(--color-cta-muted);
    max-width: 52ch;
  }

  /* Primary button — identical to the hero's primary button per the
     brief (§8 says "same style as hero"): solid accent, white 15px/600,
     11px 20px padding, 8px radius. flex-shrink: 0 keeps the button's
     label from being squeezed when the copy grows to fill the row;
     white-space: nowrap keeps the label on a single line even at
     narrow widths where flex-wrap has already dropped the button to
     its own line. */
  .btn-primary {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: #ffffff;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    line-height: 1;
    padding: 11px 20px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-button);
    text-decoration: none;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .btn-primary:hover {
    background: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }

  .btn-primary:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent-text);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* Narrow-viewport CTA — at and below ~740px the copy column's
     flex-basis (320px) plus the button's intrinsic ~253px plus the 32px
     gap no longer fit on one row, so flex-wrap drops the button onto its
     own row. The empirical wrap band ends at ~740px (measured by an
     iframe sweep of the built page): above that width the row layout
     returns cleanly, below it flex-wrap has already dropped the button.
     With justify-content: space-between and a single flex-item per row,
     the button would otherwise anchor to the left of that row leaving a
     large empty gap on its right, next to the copy that stretched to
     full width above. Stretching the button to fill the row removes
     that gap, and keeps the same visual pattern all the way down to
     the narrowest widths — the copy is a full-width block above and
     the CTA is a full-width block below. At the narrowest widths
     (roughly 320-334px) the button's content-box would still be
     narrower than the ~253px single-line label, so overriding
     white-space: nowrap to white-space: normal here lets the label
     wrap onto a second line inside the button's own box so
     scrollWidth === clientWidth on the button at every viewport width
     in scope. Above 740px the button reverts to auto width,
     white-space: nowrap on the base rule takes back over, and the row
     layout returns (copy left, button right) untouched. */
  @media (max-width: 740px) {
    .cta-panel-section .btn-primary {
      align-self: stretch;
      /* box-sizing: border-box so `width: 100%` collapses to the panel's
         content-box width including our 11px 20px padding — otherwise the
         default content-box sizing lets padding add on top and the button
         still overhangs the panel's right edge at 320-500px. */
      box-sizing: border-box;
      width: 100%;
      text-align: center;
      /* white-space: normal overrides the base rule's `nowrap` so the label
         can wrap onto a second line inside the button's own box at the
         narrowest widths (320-334px), where the ~253px single-line label
         otherwise overflows the stretched button's painted background. */
      white-space: normal;
    }
  }
</style>
